#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:13
msgid "// By default iterating over an array just gives its elements.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:15
msgid "\"The element is {letter:?}, but what's its index?\""
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:18
msgid "// `enumerate` gives both the element and its index.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:20
msgid "\"{letter:?} has index {i}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:27
msgid ""
"A nuance of Rust's iterators and `for` loops is that an iterator by default "
"does not give you the index of each element. This is fine when all you need "
"are e.g. the values in an array, but it's common to also want the index of "
"each element as you iterate."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:32
msgid ""
"`enumerate` creates an iterator that produces a tuple where the first "
"element is the index and the second element is the original iterator's item."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:35
msgid ""
"Point out that we can use a pattern for the loop variable in a `for` loop. "
"This allows us to destructure the tuple produced by `enumerate`."
msgstr ""

#: src/iterators/enumerate.md:38
msgid ""
"Note that we had to do `.iter()` first; `enumerate` is an iterator method, "
"not a method on the array type itself."
msgstr ""
